  "content": "<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><div id=\"primary\"> <article id=\"post-1339001\"><section><div id=\"\"><div><nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumbs\"><ol><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/\"><span>Science</span></a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-index/\"><span>APOD</span></a></li><li aria-current=\"page\"><span>APOD: 2026 September 16 –...</span></li></ol></nav></div><div><nav><ul><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/\">Today's APOD</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/submit/\">Submissions</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-index/\">Index</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-search/\">Search</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-calendar/\">Calendar</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-education/\">Education</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-about/\">About</a></li><li><a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/discuss_apod.php?date=260916\">Discuss</a></li></ul></nav></div></div><div>\n\n<div id=\"\"> <p>APOD</p> <p>Astronomy Picture of the Day</p> <p> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a> Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer. </p> <div><p><img width=\"3853\" height=\"4070\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=3853&amp;h=4070&amp;fit=clip&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" alt=\"Hubble and JWST image of M64. A massive spiral galaxy glows with a yellow core, surrounded by arms full of orange-brown dust and pink and blue patches of star formation. Framed by a haze of dark dust, the galaxy shines against black space dotted with a few stars.\" decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=3853&amp;h=4070&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 3853w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=284&amp;h=300&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 284w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=768&amp;h=811&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 768w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=969&amp;h=1024&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 969w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=1454&amp;h=1536&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1454w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=1939&amp;h=2048&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1939w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=379&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 379w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=568&amp;h=600&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 568w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=852&amp;h=900&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 852w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=1136&amp;h=1200&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1136w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Webb.jpg?w=1893&amp;h=2000&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1893w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 3853px) 100vw, 3853px\"><img width=\"3851\" height=\"4070\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=3851&amp;h=4070&amp;fit=clip&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" alt=\"Hubble image of M64. A massive spiral galaxy glows with a yellow core, surrounded by arms full of dark dust and pink and blue patches of star formation. Framed by a haze of dark dust, the galaxy shines against black space dotted with a few stars.\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=3851&amp;h=4070&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 3851w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=284&amp;h=300&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 284w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=768&amp;h=812&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 768w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=969&amp;h=1024&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 969w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=1453&amp;h=1536&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1453w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=1938&amp;h=2048&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1938w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=378&amp;h=399&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 378w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=568&amp;h=600&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 568w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=852&amp;h=900&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 852w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=1135&amp;h=1200&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1135w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/M64_Hubble.jpg?w=1892&amp;h=2000&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1892w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 3851px) 100vw, 3851px\"></p></div> <div> <div> <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Sometimes where <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2001-august-6-the-orbiting-hubble-space-telescope/\">Hubble</a> finds darkness, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">Webb</a> sees light. An example is today’s composite images of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/explore-the-night-sky/hubble-messier-catalog/messier-64/\">Messier 64</a> (M64), a nearby <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/types/\">spiral galaxy</a> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Eye_Galaxy\">of many names</a>. The dark band of <a href=\"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/news/dust-galaxy\">dust</a> partially blocking its bright core earned it the moniker “<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-20-m64-the-black-eye-galaxy/\">the Black Eye Galaxy</a>.” Webb’s <a href=\"https://jwst-docs.stsci.edu/jwst-mid-infrared-instrument\">Mid-InfraRed Instrument</a> (MIRI) sees that dust, shown in red, as it absorbs and re-emits light from surrounding newborn stars. These young stars are embedded in pink star-forming regions in the Hubble-only image. M64’s inner and outer gas regions <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1992Natur.360..442B/abstract\">counter-rotate</a>, creating regions of increased star formation where the two gas “currents” meet and compress. A merger between M64 and a smaller galaxy was likely the cause of the opposing motion of the outer gas. Spiral galaxies were once thought to have peaceful histories. M64 was key evidence that spiral galaxies, including the <a href=\"https://astrobites.org/2019/03/12/hunting-for-stellar-streams/\">Milky Way</a>, can and do experience mergers. Webb’s view of M64 will tell astronomers about the structure, motion, and composition of the galaxy’s dust and add context to the galaxy’s merger history and evolution.</p><p><strong>APOD's main NASA site is <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/viewtopic.php?t=45023\">moving</a>:</strong> From <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/\">apod.nasa.gov</a> to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod\">science.nasa.gov/apod</a><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> treasure </p></div> <div> <table> <tbody> <tr> <th scope=\"row\">Date:</th> <td> September 16, 2026 </td> </tr> <tr> <th scope=\"row\">Credit:</th> <td> NASA, CSA, ESA, F. Belfiore (ESO), J. Lee (STScI), A. Leroy (OSU), and D. Thilker (JHU); <em>Processing</em>: G. Kober (NASA/Catholic University) </td> </tr> <tr> <th scope=\"row\">Authors &amp; editors:</th> <td> Keighley Rockcliffe, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti </td> </tr> <tr> <th scope=\"row\">A service of:</th> <td> <a href=\"https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/astrophysics/\" target=\"_blank\">ASD</a> at <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a> / <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\" target=\"_blank\">GSFC</a>,<br><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/learn/about-science-activation/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA Science Activation</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/\" target=\"_blank\">Michigan Tech. U.</a> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </div> </div> </div>\n\n<p id=\"\"> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/random-apod/\" target=\"_self\" aria-label=\"Random APOD Generator\"> <span>Random APOD Generator</span> <svg viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"16\" r=\"16\"></circle><path d=\"M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z\"></path></svg> </a> </p> </div></section></article>\n</div></div>",
